in @prism

in @mirror/glass/ast
in @mirror/glass/ast/shape
in @mirror/glass/ast/token

# @mirror/glass/ast/shape/lambda — the `lambda` shape variant: the
# binding form for an action / function-like surface.
#
# Per the per-variant file pattern declared in @mirror/glass/ast/
# shape: variants of `shape` may land in their own sub-file when
# they want to declare structure of their own. `lambda` does —
# it carries the full function shape: name, args, return type,
# body, and an `io` annotation flag.
#
# `lambda` replaces the legacy `action` keyword (OOP-ish drift);
# FP-canonical naming aligns with the corpus convention (`λsh`
# already in use for the shell lambda). The `io` annotation rides
# as a field rather than a separate variant per the Stage 1
# decision in @mirror/glass/ast/shape:
#
#   "io is an attribute on lambda, not a separate variant."
#
# Legacy correspondence (corpus forms):
#
#   action name(arg: type) -> ret { ... }            # pure
#   io action name(arg: type) -> ret { ... }         # io-flagged
#   io serve -> imperfect { ... }                    # nullary, io
#
# At substrate altitude, all three collapse to the same variant
# with the `io` flag distinguishing the IO posture. Nullary forms
# carry an empty args list; the substrate doesn't need a separate
# nullary variant.
#
# Field rationale:
#
#   - name   : the binding name (identifier).
#   - args   : list of (name, type-name) pairs. The legacy combinator
#              `args = repeat(seq(name, optional(literal(":"),
#              whitespace, type_ref)), 0, none)` lifts here; the
#              substrate-altitude form is the pair structure.
#   - return : the return type as an identifier reference. Free
#              (unlike `property`, where return is fixed to
#              `verdict`). The identifier resolves through normal
#              type-namespace lookup.
#   - body   : a brace_block. Opaque-to-substrate at this altitude
#              (per the legacy spec `body = brace_block(repeat(any,
#              0, none))`); body-altitude lifts in later stages of
#              #94.
#   - io     : the annotation flag. `true` iff the legacy `io`
#              prefix is present. Carries the substrate-pull
#              discipline: io lambdas may touch @io; pure lambdas
#              must not.
#
# Body altitude (Reed's stop-and-report #3):
#
#   The `expr` primitive Reed's sketch references doesn't exist as
#   a typed substrate primitive yet. Reed's lean was "opaque bytes
#   for now; body altitude lives in subsequent stages." The honest
#   move: use `brace_block` (the existing @mirror/glass/ast
#   primitive). The substrate already knows brace_block; it's the
#   correct altitude for "the body's bracket-delimited bytes,
#   opaque to this declaration's measurement."
#
# Distinction from `property`:
#
#   - `property` : return fixed to `verdict`; pure by construction;
#                  no `io` field.
#   - `lambda`   : free return type; may be `io`-flagged; the
#                  general function-shaped declaration.
#
# Both share name + args + body structure; `lambda` adds `return`
# and `io`. The compounding from `property` shows: same record
# spine, additional fields where the surface demands them.

grammar @mirror/glass/ast/shape/lambda {
  # The `lambda` shape variant: full function declaration. The
  # args list follows the legacy combinator shape: pairs of
  # (identifier, identifier) where the first is the parameter name
  # and the second is the type-name reference. Return is a free
  # identifier (no verdict discipline); body is a brace_block
  # (opaque-to-substrate at this altitude); io is the annotation
  # flag.
  type lambda {
    name:   identifier,
    args:   [(identifier, identifier)],
    return: identifier,
    body:   brace_block,
    io:     bool,
  }
}

out lambda
out @mirror/glass/ast/shape/lambda
